<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eanfrith of Bernicia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/634">634</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eanfrith was king of Bernicia, the northern part of Northumbria, in 633–634, the eldest son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelfrith-of-northumbria">Æthelfrith</a>. During the reign of Edwin he lived in exile among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a>, where he fathered Talorgan, later king of the Picts from 653 to 657. After Edwin's death he returned to rule Bernicia but abandoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, and within a year he was killed by the British king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadwallon-ap-cadfan">Cadwallon of Gwynedd</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eanfrith's father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelfrith-of-northumbria">Æthelfrith</a>, was a Bernician king who also ruled Deira to the south; his mother was Acha of Deira. Æthelfrith was killed in battle around 616 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raedwald-of-east-anglia">Raedwald of East Anglia</a>, who had given refuge to Edwin, an exiled prince of Deira, and Edwin became king of Northumbria. Eanfrith, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>'s account the eldest of Æthelfrith's sons, went into exile in the north.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> writes in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecclesiastical-history-of-the-english-people">Ecclesiastical History of the English People</a> that for as long as Edwin reigned, the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelfrith-of-northumbria">Æthelfrith</a> and many of the nobility lived in banishment among the Scots or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a>, where they were instructed in the doctrine of the Scots and baptised. There Eanfrith married a Pictish princess.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Edwin was killed by the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadwallon-ap-cadfan">Cadwallon ap Cadfan</a> of Gwynedd and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda of Mercia</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hatfield-chase">Battle of Hatfield Chase</a> in October 633. Eanfrith seized the moment to return home and became king of Bernicia. He did not rule Deira, which was held by Osric, a relative of Edwin. Bede says that as soon as Eanfrith became king he renounced and lost his faith and went back to the abominations of his former idols.</p><p>Eanfrith may at first have cooperated with Cadwallon. The historian D. P. Kirby, pointing to how quickly Eanfrith was able to exploit Edwin's death, has suggested that a wide-ranging set of alliances may have joined the two men. If any such friendship existed it did not last: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> reports that Eanfrith went to Cadwallon with only twelve chosen soldiers to negotiate peace, and that Cadwallon had him killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Eanfrith's brother Oswald of Bernicia, a Christian, defeated Cadwallon and went on to rule both Bernicia and Deira. The year in which Eanfrith and Osric had reigned was later held to be so abhorrent, because of their paganism, that it was added to the length of Oswald's reign, so that the brief reigns of the two apostate kings were passed over in the reckoning of the kings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eanfrith of Bernicia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
